Copilot analysis 

Correct solution
Since your remote branch is origin/main, and your local branch is Main, the simplest fix is to rename your local branch to main if you want to match the repo convention.
But in this repo, there is already a remote branch named main, so the local rename should be:
git switch Main
git branch -M main
This will rename the current local branch Main to main.
Then push:
git push -u origin main

Why -M is the right command
git branch -M main means:
	• rename the current branch to main
	• force the rename if necessary
This is safer than git branch -m when the branch is already close to the intended name.

If you want the shortest fix
Run:
git switch Main
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

That will align your local branch with the remote branch and publish your commits.
The root issue is simply a naming mismatch: local Main vs remote main. Your repo is otherwise fine.

